Title:
SilentChrome-BOF

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- SilentChrome-BOF is a Windows tool (standalone EXE plus Cobalt Strike BOF/Aggressor wrapper) that installs Chromium persistence artifacts without driving the Chrome/Edge UI.  
- It targets Google Chrome and Microsoft Edge and supports silently seeding: an unpacked MV3 extension, a per-user Native Messaging host registration, and Isolated Web App (IWA) install records (including Direct Sockets entitlements via key-distribution components).  
- The core approach is direct manipulation of browser profile state (Preferences/Secure Preferences, Local State) and IWA-related LevelDB/component files, plus HKCU registry writes for native messaging.  
- It includes an example IWA that starts a local SOCKS5 listener via Direct Sockets, demonstrating how IWAs can be used as a post-exploitation capability container.  
- Useful for red teams/pentesters needing stealthy browser-based footholds and for defenders/researchers studying Chromium profile tampering and IWA abuse paths.  
- Interesting because it operationalizes newer Chromium features (IWA + key distribution) and avoids common “UI automation” artifacts by editing on-disk state directly.

Technical Focus:
- Chromium profile tampering (Preferences, Secure Preferences, tracked-preference MAC/super_mac)
- Native Messaging host registration via HKCU registry keys (Chrome/Edge)
- Isolated Web Apps (IWA) installation artifacts (webapp.pb, signed web bundle .swbn)
- Local State feature enablement and component registration (IwaKeyDistribution)
- LevelDB WriteBatch/log manipulation for web app records
- Direct Sockets entitlements and key-distribution protobuf generation/patching

Use Cases:
- Silent deployment of an unpacked MV3 extension for collection, auth/session workflows, or command channeling
- Registering a Native Messaging host to bridge an extension to a local executable
- Seeding an IWA into a target profile for durable app-like execution without extension UI prompts
- Establishing local pivoting via an IWA Direct Sockets SOCKS5 listener (example PoC)
- Disabling profile sync features to reduce interference/rollback from cloud sync

Keywords:
Chromium, Google Chrome, Microsoft Edge, Cobalt Strike, BOF, Aggressor Script, MV3 extension, Secure Preferences, Preferences, tracked preferences MAC, super_mac, Native Messaging, NativeMessagingHosts, HKCU registry, Isolated Web App, IWA, Direct Sockets, Local State, LevelDB, WriteBatch, webapp.pb, signed web bundle, .swbn, IwaKeyDistribution, protobuf, profile persistence